db/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1031 "Describing Planar Regions Using Set-Builder Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1033 4392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051063 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7df685b7-b3c7-5f49-ba38-fa8edde1bc9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198151 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198151 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/89/89fa15ffb9a7f6b319cf3b9ff0de4ab821a15f632d7f93618fbe99b7979260f4.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198151 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06ee94f5-8d8c-537b-83ff-69d6e351e4d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198151 #edb/ref 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx \\in \\{1,2,3\\}, \\quad y \\in \\{ -3,-2,-1,0,1,2 \\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad -3 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{ (x,y)  \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z^2}\n\\: : \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}  \n\\leq  x \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3} , \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}  \n\\leq  y \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2} \n\\Big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198152 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198152 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/45/45260087c5196cc239f21402d73169f60139bd7d2c10ee0aa1db4e1a2c56be4a.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "487a42f5-a562-5df1-9225-dbbc0046c002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198152 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\gt 2x, \\qquad y < 3.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y < 3.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y= 3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb R^2 } \\: : \\:  2x \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt } y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3 \\Big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198153 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198153 #edb/ref 1071 "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5f5f2f2e67ba0ad566b2759ca5536d248f671bf0f082d567a7f7d993772601e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88fba9fd-d05b-546c-89aa-edcf8d2fc93e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198153 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(10,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\gt 400.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 }x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25 }y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400 \\bigg\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198163 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198163 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/78/787b62fbe367feae9e4c8ed10b983a256840e3a93727fea7c1ff67cacad0129d.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198163 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56561d78-d192-5197-81f7-303abf75018f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198163 #edb/ref 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x+2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\lt x+2\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z ^2} \\: : \\: y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2 \\Big\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1039 "Describing Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198474 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d9f0da82-f79b-5056-ac99-b401eb1630c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198475 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198475 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198475 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198475 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287779 #edb/re