$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x\\le y<2\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198790 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fb/fbd778f48199acd5b731e739cb527a990ba28a8d88981d50554d135e853f8815.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dedb72cf-47cf-5e98-a478-6359d9544476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198790 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199443 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199443 #edb/ref 1061 87317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199443 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d191af84-e9e1-51b3-b4b9-018c281925db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199443 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199444 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199444 #edb/ref 1061 87318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199444 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1ab18d5-76dd-50e8-a748-69fa58e58a75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199444 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199445 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199445 #edb/ref 1061 87321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199445 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a8f59f0-1d78-52d2-aaa4-fd59600b7e56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199445 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199446 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199446 #edb/ref 1061 87324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199446 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "082a1615-eb30-5fc5-a69d-99c7441178ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199446 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199447 #edb/ref 1061 87327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199447 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c64bed7-519a-5278-8b71-573f652b4592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199447 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199551 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use set-builder notation to represent sets that lie in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, let's describe the following shaded region using set-builder-notation.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4545ab59c1bf6847ab38b09ddf8b86c3227bbc6006835aceb8637b45bff817.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, notice that a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<x<6,\\,2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that we also need to   \n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=2$), and\n\n- *include* the right edge ($x=6$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le x\\le 6,\\,2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{\n(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2\n\\: : \\:  \n\n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} \nx \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}  \n6, \n\\: \n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt} \ny\n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt}   \n5 \n\\Big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199551 #edb/ref 1065 8221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199551 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "76f96423-a638-5ff2-8797-e0e8559da636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199551 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284817 #edb/ref 1080 "\\ne " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284817 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284817 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03ae9695-f75b-5e80-9b1c-90cce0d389b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284831 #edb/ref 1080 "\\le " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04e9760d-e45c-5051-bbd0-2a36daa0f878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284843 #edb/ref 1080 "<" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284843 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284843 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06e53d49-a131-5b22-8032-7c46e048c20e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284872 #edb/ref 1080 "R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186